<p>Systems thinking comes to me so naturally that it wasn't until I saw it treated formally that I realised it was a thing. I would just have described it as "thinking".</p>
<p>Back in 2005, I was in the cafe in Borders in Stockport (which is sadly no longer there). My wife pointed over at a book in the business section and asked me if I knew what it was about. The book's title was "The Fifth Discipline". I said I didn't, but given that I'll read anything, on the way out I picked it up and flicked through it. It had a lot of strange pictures in it, but I couldn't find out what it was about except that it was called "systems thinking". "Yeah, whatevs," I remember thinking, and left.</p>
<p>Four months later, I was lent a copy to read, along with the far superior "Seeing Systems" by Barry Oshry. I'm going to claim I learnt nothing from either book. That isn't true of course, but what I learnt was basically a formal language for discussing something that was already instinctive to me.</p>
<p>I also gained an understanding that it was not for other people. I don't know how they get through life.</p>
<p>As with many other disciplines I have encountered in my life, once I'm aware that this is an "optional" skill and not integral to the way everyone thinks, I divide the world into two (or three) categories: those who do this naturally and those who don't (and those who have learnt how to do it by studying books on the subject). And I frequently suggest reading (or lend my copy of) Seeing Systems to people to help them make the switch.</p>
<p>It's not obvious to me how someone who doesn't see systems can possibly do the job that I do (building software systems). I realise this is a failing in me - there are many ways to skin a cat. But both software systems and the teams that build them are <i>systems</i> - and I don't understand how you can build one or be part of the other if you don't understand that.</p>
<p>Then again, maybe I just need to accept - as often seems to be the case - that you can do some portion of the job (say writing a few lines of code here or there), without considering how it fits into the big picture. Certainly taking that view doesn't end up stretching my imagination too much when I consider a lot of people that I have worked with.</p>
<p>If you don't see the team as a system, then you probably don't see the ways in which your interaction with the client is a vital part of that system, and so won't do it. If you don't see security as a vital part of the system, you won't pay attention to it. If you don't think about the product lifecycle and what will happen in production, you won't build in the reliability and tools that are needed for that environment.</p>
<p>But it certainly doesn't stop at my job.</p>
<p>When I am getting on or off a tram, I am not asking myself "what is best for me?" (people who ask this tend to answer "barge past everyone else") but "what is best for the system" (answer", let everyone off the tram in an orderly fashion, then people get on in an orderly fashion and move away from the doors").</p>
<p>Another "go to" example (in part because I spend so much of my life there) is coffee shops. There are a lot of things that need doing at the same time: taking orders, making drinks, clearing tables, restocking supplies, washing up; the list goes on.</p>
<p>As a customer, each of us wants <b>our</b> order completed right now. But that isn't reasonable, and it's interesting to see what is prioritized and when. It seems obvious that taking orders should be prioritized, but the theory of constraints tells us that that is not necessarily so; but in general, it is possible to postpone clearing and restocking to times when there aren't customers waiting. I discuss this in more detail elsewhere. The important thing here is that what is important is the health of the <i>system</i>. Each order and customer matters, but need to be considered in a broader scope.</p>
<p>A lot of the time there are social norms around things (like boarding trams). It's interesting just how many of these norms (in England, at least) reflect a systems view of the problem. Why is that? Because society is a system, and some people at some time did the necessary thinking to say "this system would work better if...".  All of which has now been replaced by "but I want to..." or just "me, me, me" with no apparent thinking involved at all.</p>
<p>One more reason I feel out of place in this world.</p>
